      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: add standard hosting profiles",
        "body": "Related RFC: https://github.com/openclaw/rfcs/pull/37\n\nDepends on: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/104018\n\n> **Stacked draft:** this branch contains exact readiness head `17b34f792d8` from PR 104018 followed by eight profile-only commits. Until PR 104018 lands, GitHub's aggregate diff against `main` includes both layers. After it lands, this PR reduces to the Hosting Profiles work described below.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenClaw runs locally, in containers, behind identity proxies, and as a controller for paired execution targets, but those deployment labels are not reproducible support contracts. Operators currently spread the intended posture across startup flags, environment, config, container probes, adapter code, and private checklists. A Gateway process can be alive and logging while it still cannot safely serve its intended workload.\n\nThis change makes that distinction explicit. A selected profile names the posture OpenClaw is expected to satisfy, and the canonical readiness result remains `503` with stable reasons until every required runtime fact is true.\n\n## Standard Hosting Profiles\n\nProfiles are named, release-tested presets over the canonical readiness conditions from PR 104018. They do not introduce a second health system.\n\n| Profile | Supported posture | Additional required readiness evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `local` | Explicit foreground or local Gateway posture | Universal Gateway readiness and writable workspace |\n| `container` | Directly reachable container listener | Non-loopback effective listener and writable mounted workspace |\n| `reverse-proxy` | Gateway behind a trusted identity proxy | Trusted-proxy auth mode, identity header, trusted source, and writable workspace |\n| `node-mode` | Gateway controlling one or more execution targets | Approved pairing, connected target, approved command surface, live control channel, and writable workspace |\n\nProfiles are opt-in. Without a selected profile, OpenClaw preserves the readiness baseline from PR 104018 and does not silently add profile conditions. When selected, precedence is:\n\n```text\ngateway startup argument > environment > openclaw.json\n```\n\nOperators select a profile through `hosting.profile`, `OPENCLAW_HOSTING_PROFILE`, or `gateway run --hosting-profile`. Invalid startup values fail before destructive runtime reset/force behavior. Results include a logical runtime ID and a unique process/container incarnation ID.\n\nProfiles validate effective runtime state. They do not generate or repair configuration, encode scheduler retry policy, introduce Lobster/OCC concepts, or replace direct readiness-condition selection.\n\n## Why This Helps Operators\n\nDocker, Kubernetes, systemd, OCC, and downstream hosts can consume the ordinary `/readyz` contract instead of reconstructing readiness from logs or process liveness. A host receives `200` only when the selected posture can serve work; expected deployment failures return `503` and a named condition explaining what remains unmet.\n\nThis matters most for partial startup. For example, node-mode remains non-ready when its Gateway is running, and remains non-ready even after a node transport connects, until the node command surface is explicitly approved. The host does not send traffic merely because a process exists.\n\nMaintainers gain a bounded support matrix for reproducing deployment issues against a named posture rather than an open-ended configuration claim.\n\n## Implementation Stack\n\nReadiness dependency head: `17b34f792d8`\n\nProfile-only commits:\n\n1. `642bed18093` standard `local`, `container`, and `reverse-proxy` profiles\n2. `b9121934a7f` product-neutral `node-mode` readiness\n3. `2b10b218279` runtime activation identity\n4. `81f787820da` packaged profile conformance and release wiring\n5. `05bbe94f8d5` startup validation and lifecycle safety\n6. `ab612666315` host-provisioned workspace proof\n7. `630684819c8` explicit node approval readiness proof\n8. `f54affa3983` explicit opt-in activation and upgrade-safe unprofiled proof\n\nProfile-only diff: 32 files, 1,343 insertions, and 5 deletions, including docs and tests.\n\nFork review slices: [#18](https://github.com/giodl73-repo/openclaw/pull/18), [#19](https://github.com/giodl73-repo/openclaw/pull/19), [#42](https://github.com/giodl73-repo/openclaw/pull/42), and [#21](https://github.com/giodl73-repo/openclaw/pull/21). [Fork PR #94](https://github.com/giodl73-repo/openclaw/pull/94) preserves the profile-only diff against the readiness branch.\n\n## Verification\n\nExact source head: `f54affa3983b4803e424e098cf1b359c42481704`\n\nEnvironment: isolated Ubuntu 24.04 checkout, Node 24.15+, Docker Linux/amd64. No Lobster, OCC, tenant service, or unpublished local dependency was involved.\n\nFocused source verification:\n\n- 273 focused assertions passed across the changed profile, runtime, node-mode, config, CLI, planner, release, Gateway startup, and readiness files.\n- The profile-added `gateway.test.ts` startup assertion passes alone. In the broad file, the unrelated `sessions.abort` timeout reproduces on the exact readiness base; the workspace-plugin count assertion is order-sensitive and passes when selected directly on both heads.\n- Shell syntax, JavaScript syntax, Oxfmt on 30 files, Oxlint on 26 files, and `git diff --check` pass.\n- Codex review of the eight profile-only commits against readiness head `17b34f792d8` found no actionable correctness regression.\n\nPackage-installed Docker verification:\n\n```text\nNODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=16384 \\\n  bash scripts/e2e/hosting-profiles-docker.sh\n```\n\nThe exact-head run exited `0`, built the package and functional Docker image, reported `OpenClaw package tarball integrity passed`, and completed with `Hosting profiles Docker E2E passed`.\n\nImage: `sha256:20d507b613b346e1165add88234eb00390ea6d1b086970dd3589dd3f41654175`\n\n| Scenario | Expected and observed result |\n| --- | --- |\n| No profile selected | Existing readiness baseline stays `200`; no profile-only conditions are added |\n| Explicit local profile | `200` with `ProfileSelected` and writable-workspace evidence |\n| Container with non-loopback listener | `200` |\n| Container with loopback listener | `503 ContainerGatewayLoopback` |\n| Correct trusted reverse proxy | `200` |\n| Reverse proxy without trusted-proxy auth | `503 TrustedProxyAuthMissing` |\n| Node-mode with no node | `503` with missing pairing/target/approval/control-channel reasons |\n| Node connected but command surface unapproved | `503 NodePairingPending` |\n| Existing `openclaw nodes approve` flow completed | `200` |\n| Writable host-provisioned workspace | `200` |\n| Workspace filled to `ENOSPC` | `503 WorkspaceStorageFull` |\n| Workspace capacity restored | `200` |\n\nThe PR remains draft and stacked on PR 104018. Readiness can be reviewed and landed independently; this PR is the separate product/support decision for named release-tested postures.\n",
        "branch": "user/giodl/standard-hosting-profiles-upstream",
